# T-1122: TyConDef.annotation population from @[...] on a type alias declaration key.
# eval_type_annotation_property_dict extracts literal values (strings, ints, floats, bools)
# from the type-alias-level @[...] PropertyDict and stores them in TyConDef.annotation.
#
# For a named-field constructor with @[...], the annotation is populated into
# TyConDef.annotation (type-checker layer). At runtime, the constructor function itself
# carries the annotation via FnAnnotation.extra (set up by build_constructor_return_ann,
# T-1052/T-1053), so annotation-of on the constructor returns the annotation dict.
#
# This test exercises T-1122 by declaring a named-field constructor with:
#   - A string annotation field (role: "primary") — captured as String value
#   - An int annotation field (priority: 1) — captured as Int value
# Both are literal values and pass through eval_type_annotation_property_dict.
#
# annotation-of(Widget) returns {"role": "primary", "priority": 1}.
# The test verifies both fields are present with correct values.
# Absence of === warn asserts the named-field constructor annotation path is clean.
[MyLibType: [type [Widget@[role: "primary"  priority: 1] name: String]]]
[ann: [annotation-of MyLibType.Widget]]
[result: [and [= [get "role" ann] "primary"] [= [get "priority" ann] 1]]]
=== out
Dict({"result": Bool(true)})
